0.099001  2026-11-12 08:40:12 CET
   - Same as 0.099-TRIAL, plus:
   - Model::SAML2 requires Net::SAML2 0.86: older versions silently
     skip the check on the Issuer of the assertion. SAML2 tests skip
     with older versions
   - Tests use the cheapest Argon2 settings where the cost of password
     checks is not under test (slow testers hit timeouts), and no
     longer warn about experimental signatures on perl < 5.36

0.099  2026-11-11 22:35:17 CET
   BREAKING CHANGES
   - Account records use key "username" instead of "name" (Model::Hash
     input and save_user, Model::Db default username_column), and
     load_user_by_name is now load_user_by_username (Role::Local)
   - The user identifier kept in the session is now "provider/uid", so
     that users stay tied to the provider that authenticated them;
     sessions from previous versions are considered logged out
   - Every provider must implement handles_username. The first provider
     (in the order given) that claims a username owns it, and only the
     owner validates credentials for it. A SAML2 provider with default
     settings claims every username, so it shadows any provider after it
   - Every refused credentials login throws the same 401 (wrong
     password, unknown username, owner taking no credentials), instead
     of letting later providers try
   - Every provider with load_user must implement tamed_user_record,
     returning what may reach current_user; the Model checks this at
     construction and gives out a deep copy (records must be plain
     data). Role::Local leaves out key_for_secret ("secret" by default)
     and protected_keys; providers' own load_user still return them
   - Remaps can neither read nor change the protected keys: to keep the
     hash in a column not named "secret", set key_for_secret instead of
     remapping it
   - Model::SAML2 has no validate_user any more: SAML2 users only log
     in through the IdP
   - encode_name and decode_name are functions in
     MojoX::Authentication::Util, no longer Model methods
   - Model::SAML2 cache keys include the provider name: users cached by
     a previous version must log in again
   - The Model writes each provider's name (the one given in the hash
     form, or unnamed-N) into the provider, so name is now read-write in
     Model::Hash, Model::Db and Model::SAML2. A custom provider with a
     name method must accept being renamed, or the Model throws a 400
   - ctr_credentials_login and ctr_logout require a valid CSRF token
     (csrf_token parameter, e.g. %= csrf_field in the form, or the
     X-CSRF-Token header): forms without it can no longer log in or out
   - Require Mojolicious 9.20 and Mojolicious::Plugin::Authentication
     1.39. Net::SAML2 and MojoX::MojoDbWrap are now optional
     (recommended), the latter in version 0.100 or later

   SECURITY
   - With secrets_are_cleartext, records without a secret got the hash
     of an empty password, so anyone could log in with an empty one
   - The same uid in two providers (e.g. local and SAML2) gave whoever
     came first, on every request
   - SAML2 request ids are single use, so a SAMLResponse cannot be
     replayed
   - Response times no longer tell known usernames from unknown ones:
     every attempt costs one password check
   - Accounts without a secret (or with an empty one) were refused at
     once, without checking any password, so response times told that
     they exist: they now cost one password check too. To disable an
     account, empty its secret instead of writing a marker (e.g. "!")
   - The stored password hash is no longer written to the log (trace
     level) after a successful login
   - Remaps could leave the password hash under another key (e.g. the
     "userpass" column remapped to "secret"), or copy it out of
     "secret", and so expose it in current_user, templates and logs
   - Two SAML2 providers sharing a cache (e.g. the same database table)
     shared its entries: a user of one IdP could get the attributes of
     a user with the same NameID at the other, and a logout through one
     wiped the other's user
   - SAML2 assertions are refused unless their Issuer is the IdP's
     entityID: only the signature was checked, so another issuer holding
     the same signing key (e.g. another tenant of a multi-tenant IdP
     platform) could log anybody in
   - SAML2 assertions without a NameID (or with an empty one) are
     refused: all such users shared one session id and one cache entry,
     each seeing the data of whoever logged in last
   - Usernames from the login form and user identifiers from the IdP
     were logged verbatim, so a newline in them could forge log lines:
     characters outside printable ASCII are now logged as \x{..}
   - Refused SAML2 responses were logged with the error that refused
     them, verbatim: text outside the part signed by the IdP (e.g. the
     status) could forge log lines, and a stack trace could write the
     whole response (megabytes) to the log at the error level. The
     error line is now escaped and cut; the full message goes to trace
   - The credentials login and the logout had no CSRF protection: a
     hostile page could log the user in as somebody else (login CSRF)
     or log them out
   - SAML2 caches purge expired entries, so abandoned logins (or anybody
     looping over the login route) no longer grow memory or tables
     without limit

   FIXES
   - The default "not_ok" route was misspelled
   - Use the object's provider_name; ctr_logout logs the user and always
     redirects when asked to
   - Model::load_user returns nothing for unknown users
   - Model::Hash never modifies the db it is given and rejects invalid
     input; Model::Db works with MojoX::MojoDbWrap 0.100
   - SAML2: request_ttl is honoured, cache keys use user_key, logout
     wipes the right user, no temporary file is leaked
   - SAML2: error statuses from the IdP are reported as 403 naming the
     status, and the idp can be given as an XML string
   - The SAML2 welcome message falls back to the uid
   - Fix the example application; its comments warn that its secret and
     SP key are public, so real applications must set their own

   OTHER
   - A provider given as { instance => ... } gets that hash as second
     argument of update_from_config, to pick parameters meant for it
   - Model::Hash and Model::Db save_user share Role::Local's user_for_save
   - Documentation: fix the op/backwards_op names in Role::Remap's
     example; owner rules, user identifiers, rate limiting of the
     credentials login route, SAML2::Hash only suiting single-process
     applications (use a shared cache with hypnotoad or prefork),
     the format of SAML2 remaps (the one of Role::Remap)
   - Many more tests, including offline signed SAML2 responses and an
     optional live round trip against mocksaml.com (MOCKSAML=1)
   - Remove dead code and unused imports

